<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!-- TemporalToggles — the PastCast / NowCast / ForeCast temporal features as three big square keys.

       ┌──────────┐┌──────────┐┌──────────┐┌────┐  ┐ a MODE key is SQUARE and as tall as the bar's
       │    ↺     ││   ((•))  ││    ☁     ││  •  │  │ content row: height comes from the bar, width
       │ PastCast ││ NowCast  ││ ForeCast ││  •  │  │ from the height (code-behind), so both follow
       └──────────┘└──────────┘└──────────┘│  •  │  │ the bar and can't go stale
          ^           ^           ^        └────┘  ┘ lit = that mode is ACTIVE (a projection of its
          │           │           │           ^      subsystem, not a stored flag), so the lit
          │           │           │           │      state can't drift from reality
          │           │           │           └── their shared settings panel (a LATCH: lit = the
          │           │           │               Timeframe window is open). NAMELESS and HALF
          │           │           │               WIDTH — full height, so it stays part of the
          │           │           │               row; half width, so it can't read as a fourth
          │           │           └── SPC outlooks      mode. The one such key in the bar.
          │           └── live radar / current alerts
          └── historical replay

       All four sit on ONE spacing (the row's ColumnSpacing) — no extra gap before the dots.

     A MODE key is a GLYPH OVER A NAME and nothing else — it is a mode switch, not a readout. A third tier
     carrying live status (replay date / warning counts / outlook day) was built and REMOVED: it made the
     keys read as dashboard tiles when their whole job is "which timeframe am I in". Don't re-add it;
     that state belongs in the Timeframe panel. Whether the KEY is lit is the only state it reports.

     ⚠️ THE THREE NAMED KEYS AND THE ELLIPSIS ARE DIFFERENT SPECIES, sitting together on purpose. The three
     are MODE toggles (what is on the MAP); the ellipsis is a WINDOW latch (what is on the SCREEN) — the
     same contract as Sites and Settings on the bar's right edge, which is where it started. It moved here
     because the panel it opens configures these three modes and nothing else, and that adjacency is what
     makes it findable at all. The gap before it, and its having no name, are what keep it from reading as
     a fourth mode.

     ⚠️ The glyphs are a REVERSAL of the older "text pills, not icon-over-label stacks" rule, made
     deliberately: that rule came from a SMALL pill, where it was icon OR word and the word won. A key
     this size fits both, so the trade no longer applies. Codepoints were picked by rendering candidates
     in the actual font — a wrong one ships as an empty box, so verify visually before swapping one.

     ⚠️ A KEY TOGGLES ITS MODE, AND NOTHING ELSE. Unlit → click turns the mode on; LIT → click turns it OFF.
     That is all a lit toggle has ever meant anywhere, and getting back to it is the whole reason the
     Timeframe window exists.
     HISTORY, do not undo: these keys used to OWN a window each — a click activated the mode AND opened its
     settings window, and clicking a lit key flipped that window instead of turning the mode off. The
     window's open state appeared nowhere on the key, so one click did two different things depending on
     state you could not see: arm NowCast by clicking a site marker (mode on, panel never shown) or close
     the ForeCast window, and the only route back to the panel was a key that looked like it would switch
     the feature off. The panels are now TABS of ONE window with its OWN key — the ellipsis at the end of
     this row, an honest window latch. Two families of key, still: the named ones say what is on the MAP,
     the latches say what is on the SCREEN. They just no longer live in two separate clusters.
     Turning a mode ON still opens the Timeframe panel on that mode's tab, so the common path stays one
     click. Turning a mode off leaves the panel alone — it belongs to its own key now.
     You can also leave a mode by choosing another (Past is exclusive with Now/Fore; Now and Fore coexist).
     The click routes through MapViewModel.ToggleTemporalMode; the key's lit state binds OneWay to
     MapViewModel.IsPastCast/IsNowCast/IsForeCast (projections of the subsystems).

     THE LOOK IS NOT HERE — it is BarKeyToggleStyle in Controls/Styles.xaml (lit = an ACCENT BORDER over a
     face raised one surface level, deliberately not the default toggle's accent-filled block). This file
     owns the key's CONTENT and arrangement; that style owns its states. Change the look there.
     ⚠️ That style is SHARED with MainWindow's window buttons, which are the same glyph-over-name key —
     a change there shows up on both clusters, which is the point (one bar, one language).

     The keys carry the FULL names (PastCast / NowCast / ForeCast) so the display vocabulary matches the
     one the code uses everywhere. They sit at the bottom bar's exact HORIZONTAL CENTER — where the radar
     console used to be — pinned there by MainWindow's [*, Auto, *] bar grid: this control is the Auto
     column between two equal stars, so neither the console on the left nor the window buttons on the
     right can push it off centre, however wide they get (quad adds product chips left; the keys hold).
     ⚠️ Keep this control's own WIDTH content-sized and symmetric — a horizontal stretch or a one-sided
     margin here would re-introduce the drift the bar grid exists to prevent. HEIGHT is the opposite: it
     must STRETCH (here and at the host), because the row's height is what sizes the squares.

     SIZING — the keys are square and adaptive, not a fixed size. Height comes from the bar's content
     row (stretch); the code-behind mirrors that onto Width, scales the glyph off it, and MEASURES the
     longest name to pick the one font size that fits all three (the names are long enough that a
     height-derived size would overflow the square, and sizing each key to its own text would render
     "NowCast" bigger than "ForeCast"). So the keys grow with the bar — quad adds a chip row and the
     squares follow — with no magic numbers to keep in sync with a layout that moves. Tuning knobs are
     consts at the top of the code-behind. The ellipsis key is the SAME square but takes
     BarKeyMetrics.SoloGlyphRatio, since it has no name to share the square with.
     Bound to the coordinator MapViewModel. -->
<UserControl x:Class="Anvil.Controls.Composites.TemporalToggles"
             xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             xmlns:d="http://schemas.microsoft.com/expression/blend/2008"
             xmlns:mc="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/markup-compatibility/2006"
             mc:Ignorable="d">

    <!-- Stretch, NOT Center: the row's height is what the keys are measured against, so this has to
         fill the bar's content row for them to reach full height. The host must stretch it too. -->
    <Grid x:Name="PillRow"
          VerticalAlignment="Stretch"
          ColumnSpacing="8"
          SizeChanged="OnPillRowSizeChanged">
        <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
            <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/>
            <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/>
            <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/>
            <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/> <!-- the ellipsis: their shared panel -->
        </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>

        <!-- PASTCAST — glyph E81C, a clock with a counter-clockwise arrow: history/rewind. -->
        <ToggleButton x:Name="PastPill"
                      Grid.Column="0"
                      Style="{StaticResource BarKeyToggleStyle}"
                      ToolTipService.ToolTip="PastCast — replay a historical radar window (click again to turn it off)"
                      IsChecked="{x:Bind ViewModel.IsPastCast, Mode=OneWay}"
                      Click="OnPastClick">
            <Grid Margin="3,0" RowSpacing="2">
                <Grid.RowDefinitions>
                    <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/> <!-- glyph -->
                    <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/> <!-- name -->
                </Grid.RowDefinitions>
                <FontIcon x:Name="PastGlyph" Grid.Row="0"
                          FontFamily="Segoe Fluent Icons,Segoe MDL2 Assets"
                          Glyph="&#xE81C;"/>
                <TextBlock x:Name="PastName" Grid.Row="1"
                           Text="PastCast"
                           HorizontalAlignment="Center"/>
            </Grid>
        </ToggleButton>

        <!-- NOWCAST — glyph E93E, a dot radiating signal waves: live. Deliberately NOT the tower (EC05),
             which reads as "a radar site" and would collide with the on-map site markers' language. -->
        <ToggleButton x:Name="NowPill"
                      Grid.Column="1"
                      Style="{StaticResource BarKeyToggleStyle}"
                      ToolTipService.ToolTip="NowCast — live radar / current conditions (click again to turn it off)"
                      IsChecked="{x:Bind ViewModel.IsNowCast, Mode=OneWay}"
                      Click="OnNowClick">
            <Grid Margin="3,0" RowSpacing="2">
                <Grid.RowDefinitions>
                    <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/>
                    <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/>
                </Grid.RowDefinitions>
                <FontIcon x:Name="NowGlyph" Grid.Row="0"
                          FontFamily="Segoe Fluent Icons,Segoe MDL2 Assets"
                          Glyph="&#xE93E;"/>
                <TextBlock x:Name="NowName" Grid.Row="1"
                           Text="NowCast"
                           HorizontalAlignment="Center"/>
            </Grid>
        </ToggleButton>

        <!-- FORECAST — glyph E753, a cloud: the weather-outlook idiom. -->
        <ToggleButton x:Name="ForePill"
                      Grid.Column="2"
                      Style="{StaticResource BarKeyToggleStyle}"
                      ToolTipService.ToolTip="ForeCast — SPC outlooks (click again to turn it off)"
                      IsChecked="{x:Bind ViewModel.IsForeCast, Mode=OneWay}"
                      Click="OnForeClick">
            <Grid Margin="3,0" RowSpacing="2">
                <Grid.RowDefinitions>
                    <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/>
                    <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/>
                </Grid.RowDefinitions>
                <FontIcon x:Name="ForeGlyph" Grid.Row="0"
                          FontFamily="Segoe Fluent Icons,Segoe MDL2 Assets"
                          Glyph="&#xE753;"/>
                <TextBlock x:Name="ForeName" Grid.Row="1"
                           Text="ForeCast"
                           HorizontalAlignment="Center"/>
            </Grid>
        </ToggleButton>

        <!-- ===== THE PANEL KEY — an ellipsis, and the ONLY nameless key in the bar =====
             It opens the Timeframe window (PastCast / NowCast / ForeCast as three tabs), which is why it
             sits HERE and not in MainWindow's right cluster where it started: that panel configures these
             three modes and nothing else, so it belongs against them. The right cluster keeps what is
             genuinely global (panes, sites, settings).

             ⚠️ AN ELLIPSIS IS THE POINT. Three dots are the universal "there is more behind this" mark, and
             placed hard against the trio it reads as "more, for THESE" — which is precisely what it is.
             That adjacency is what teaches the panel's existence; a named key over in the right cluster had
             to be found first.

             ⚠️ THE DOTS RUN VERTICALLY AND ARE DRAWN, not a font glyph. Vertical because the key is HALF the
             width of a mode key (BarKeyMetrics.NarrowWidthFor) — a horizontal ellipsis would crowd its own
             edges in that space, while a vertical one has the height to breathe and reads as a divider-ish
             pip strip rather than a squashed word. Drawn (three Ellipses) for the same reason the pane key's
             icons are drawn: it removes the "verify this codepoint renders" risk entirely, and it scales off
             the measured side like everything else in the row.

             ⚠️ HALF WIDTH IS THE SUBORDINATION. Same HEIGHT as its neighbours — it is part of the row, and
             its height still comes from the shared stretch, never from a set value — but half the width, so
             it cannot read as a fourth mode however long you look at it. Width and dot size both come from
             Layout/BarKeyMetrics so they track the bar with nothing to keep in sync by hand.

             ⚠️ NAMELESS, deliberately, and the ONE exception to the bar's mark-over-a-name rule. That rule
             exists because a lone mark has to be LEARNED (a globe, a tower, a gear mean nothing until you
             know what is behind them) — an ellipsis is the one mark that doesn't, and naming it would be
             worse: any word for it ("More", "Panel", "Timeframe") reads as a FOURTH MODE in a row of three,
             which is the one thing this key must not look like. No name is what keeps it subordinate.
             Its dots therefore take BarKeyMetrics.SoloDrawnIconRatio — the mark has the key to itself, not
             the fraction a named key leaves over.

             ⚠️ IT IS STILL A LATCH, not an action: lit = the panel is open, click = close it. Same contract
             as Sites and Settings, so the two-families rule survives the move — this key just moved house,
             it did not change species.

             ⚠️ CENTRING: this key is INSIDE the centred cluster, so the bar's midpoint now falls in the
             middle of FOUR keys, not three. The trio therefore sits a little left of dead centre. That was
             the trade for adjacency and it is deliberate — do not "fix" it by hanging this key outside the
             Auto column, which would put it back in a star column and let it drift with the console.

             ⚠️ ONE spacing for all four keys — the row's ColumnSpacing, nothing extra here. An added gap
             before this key was tried and removed: it made the group look like "three keys, then a stray",
             and the half width already does the subordinating without a hole in the row. -->
        <!-- ⚠️ No Margin and no Width here: the code-behind owns both (the row's inset, and half the
             measured side), exactly as it does for the trio. Two owners would fight on the first
             SizeChanged. -->
        <ToggleButton x:Name="PanelKey"
                      Grid.Column="3"
                      Style="{StaticResource BarKeyToggleStyle}"
                      ToolTipService.ToolTip="Settings for the active timeframe — PastCast, NowCast and ForeCast"
                      IsChecked="{x:Bind ViewModel.IsTemporalWindowOpen, Mode=TwoWay}">
            <!-- Three drawn dots, stacked. Sizes + spacing come from the code-behind; the brush is fixed
                 because Shapes do not inherit the content Foreground the template's states drive — the same
                 reason the pane key's rectangles carry their own. -->
            <StackPanel x:Name="PanelDots" VerticalAlignment="Center" HorizontalAlignment="Center">
                <Ellipse x:Name="Dot1" Fill="{ThemeResource TextFillColorSecondaryBrush}"/>
                <Ellipse x:Name="Dot2" Fill="{ThemeResource TextFillColorSecondaryBrush}"/>
                <Ellipse x:Name="Dot3" Fill="{ThemeResource TextFillColorSecondaryBrush}"/>
            </StackPanel>
        </ToggleButton>
    </Grid>
</UserControl>
